### What is @MappedSuperclass?

`@MappedSuperclass` is a non-entity base class that holds shared mapping information. You put common fields -- an id strategy, audit columns like `createdAt` and `updatedAt`, a `@Version` column -- on this base class, and each `@Entity` subclass inherits those fields as columns in its own table. The base class is not an entity: there is no table for it, you cannot write `em.find(Auditable.class, id)`, you cannot run `SELECT a FROM Auditable a`, and no relationship can target it.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class Auditable {

    @Column(name = "created_at", updatable = false)
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @Column(name = "updated_at")
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;

    @Column(name = "created_by")
    private String createdBy;

    @Version
    private Long version;

    @PrePersist
    protected void onCreate() {
        this.createdAt = LocalDateTime.now();
        this.updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
        this.createdBy = "SYSTEM";
    }

    @PreUpdate
    protected void onUpdate() {
        this.updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }

    // getters
}
```

Every entity that extends `Auditable` gets `created_at`, `updated_at`, `created_by`, and `version` columns in its own table, plus automatic stamping via the lifecycle callbacks.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account extends Auditable {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId; // SAV2001

    @Column(name = "holder_name")
    private String holderName; // Priya

    private BigDecimal balance;
}

@Entity
@Table(name = "loan_applications")
public class LoanApplication extends Auditable {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "loan_id")
    private String loanId;

    @Column(name = "applicant_name")
    private String applicantName; // Rajesh

    @Column(name = "loan_amount")
    private BigDecimal loanAmount;
}
```

```text
accounts table:              loan_applications table:
account_id | holder_name    loan_id | applicant_name | loan_amount
balance    | created_at             | created_at     | updated_at
updated_at | created_by             | created_by     | version
version    |                        |

Both tables have audit columns, but there is no "auditable" table.
```

### What is @Inheritance?

`@Inheritance` creates a genuine polymorphic entity hierarchy. The base class is itself an `@Entity` and carries `@Inheritance(strategy = ...)`. All subclasses are entities. The base type is queryable: `SELECT a FROM Account a` returns SavingsAccount and CurrentAccount rows together. An association like `@ManyToOne Account account` can hold either subtype.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "account_type")
public abstract class Account {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    protected String accountId;

    @Column(name = "holder_name")
    protected String holderName;

    protected BigDecimal balance;
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("SAVINGS")
public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Column(name = "interest_rate")
    private BigDecimal interestRate;
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("CURRENT")
public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    @Column(name = "overdraft_limit")
    private BigDecimal overdraftLimit;
}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import java.util.List;

public class AccountService {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public AccountService(EntityManager em) { this.em = em; }

    public List<Account> findAllAccounts() {
        // Polymorphic query -- returns both SavingsAccount and CurrentAccount
        return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT a FROM Account a", Account.class)
            .getResultList();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
[SavingsAccount{SAV2001, Priya, 50000.00, rate=6.5},
 CurrentAccount{CUR3001, Rajesh, 120000.00, limit=50000.00}]
```

### Why does JPA need both mechanisms?

The two mechanisms solve different problems. @MappedSuperclass is purely a code-reuse mechanism for mappings. You want to avoid copy-pasting `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, and `version` across 30 entities, so you pull them into one base class. The subtypes are not related domain concepts -- an Account and a LoanApplication have nothing in common except audit fields.

@Inheritance models a real domain relationship. SavingsAccount and CurrentAccount are genuinely different kinds of Account. Your business logic needs to treat them uniformly sometimes ("list all accounts") and specifically other times ("calculate interest only for savings"). That requires polymorphic queries, polymorphic associations, and runtime type dispatch -- all of which only @Inheritance provides.

```java
// @MappedSuperclass use case -- unrelated entities sharing audit fields
// Account and LoanApplication are not "kinds of Auditable"
// You never query "all auditables"

// @Inheritance use case -- related domain types
// SavingsAccount IS an Account
// You DO query "all accounts" and get both types back
```

### How is @MappedSuperclass different from @Inheritance?

This comparison table captures every important difference.

| Feature | @MappedSuperclass | @Inheritance |
|---|---|---|
| Base class is an entity? | No | Yes |
| Base class has a table? | No | Yes (depends on strategy) |
| Polymorphic queries? | Not possible | Yes -- query base, get all subtypes |
| Polymorphic associations? | Not possible | Yes -- @ManyToOne to base type |
| em.find(Base.class, id)? | Not possible | Works, returns correct subtype |
| Purpose | Share column mappings | Model a real type hierarchy |
| Subclass tables | Each fully independent | Linked by strategy (single, joined, table-per-class) |
| Lifecycle callbacks? | Yes, inherited by subclasses | Yes, inherited by subclasses |
| @AttributeOverride? | Yes, subclass can override columns | Not directly supported |
| Strategy choice needed? | No | Yes -- SINGLE_TABLE, JOINED, TABLE_PER_CLASS |

```java
// Trying to query a @MappedSuperclass -- FAILS
// em.createQuery("SELECT a FROM Auditable a"); // compile error or runtime error

// Querying an @Inheritance base -- WORKS
List<Account> all = em.createQuery(
    "SELECT a FROM Account a", Account.class)
    .getResultList();
// Returns SavingsAccount and CurrentAccount rows together
```

### What are the rules and constraints?

Rules for @MappedSuperclass:

- The class must be annotated with `@MappedSuperclass`, not with `@Entity`.
- It cannot have `@Table` because it has no table.
- It can define `@Id`, `@Version`, `@Column`, lifecycle callbacks, and even `@ManyToOne` relationships that subclasses inherit.
- Subclasses can override inherited column names with `@AttributeOverride`.
- Each subclass is a completely independent entity at the database level.

Rules for @Inheritance:

- The base class must be annotated with both `@Entity` and `@Inheritance`.
- You must choose a strategy: `SINGLE_TABLE` (default), `JOINED`, or `TABLE_PER_CLASS`.
- `SINGLE_TABLE` requires a `@DiscriminatorColumn` (auto-generated if omitted).
- All subclasses must also be `@Entity`.
- The base class must have an `@Id`.

```java
// Rules demonstration
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class Auditable {
    // Can define @Id here
    // Can define @Version here
    // Can define lifecycle callbacks
    // Cannot have @Table
    // Cannot be queried
}

@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "account_type")
public abstract class Account {
    @Id private String accountId;
    // Must have @Id
    // Must choose strategy
    // Can be queried polymorphically
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

**Mistake 1: Trying to query a @MappedSuperclass.**

```java
// WRONG -- Auditable is not an entity, cannot be queried
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class Auditable {
    @Column(name = "created_at")
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
}

// This throws an exception at runtime:
// em.createQuery("SELECT a FROM Auditable a");
// IllegalArgumentException: Auditable is not a mapped entity
```

```java
// CORRECT -- query the concrete entity subclass instead
List<Account> accounts = em.createQuery(
    "SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.createdAt > :date", Account.class)
    .setParameter("date", LocalDateTime.now().minusDays(30))
    .getResultList();
```

**Mistake 2: Using @Inheritance when you only need shared fields.**

```java
// WRONG -- Account and LoanApplication are unrelated domain concepts
// Do not force them into one inheritance hierarchy
@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
public abstract class Auditable {
    @Id private Long id;
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
}

@Entity public class Account extends Auditable { ... }
@Entity public class LoanApplication extends Auditable { ... }
// Now Account and LoanApplication share one table with many nullable columns!
```

```java
// CORRECT -- use @MappedSuperclass for shared fields
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class Auditable {
    @Column(name = "created_at")
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
}

@Entity @Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account extends Auditable { @Id private String accountId; }

@Entity @Table(name = "loan_applications")
public class LoanApplication extends Auditable { @Id private String loanId; }
// Each has its own clean table with audit columns
```

**Mistake 3: Forgetting that @ManyToOne cannot reference a @MappedSuperclass.**

```java
// WRONG -- cannot reference a @MappedSuperclass in a relationship
@Entity
public class Transaction {
    @Id private Long id;
    @ManyToOne
    private Auditable relatedEntity; // Auditable is not an entity!
}
```

```java
// CORRECT -- reference a concrete entity
@Entity
public class Transaction {
    @Id private Long id;
    @ManyToOne
    private Account account; // Account is an @Entity
}
```

### Can you combine @MappedSuperclass and @Inheritance?

Yes, and this is the clean answer for most banking applications. Use @MappedSuperclass for audit fields and @Inheritance for real domain hierarchies.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

// Layer 1: shared audit fields (not an entity)
@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class Auditable {
    @Column(name = "created_at", updatable = false)
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @Column(name = "updated_at")
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;

    @Version
    private Long version;

    @PrePersist
    protected void onCreate() {
        this.createdAt = LocalDateTime.now();
        this.updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }

    @PreUpdate
    protected void onUpdate() {
        this.updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }
}

// Layer 2: real entity hierarchy (IS an entity)
@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "account_type")
public abstract class Account extends Auditable {
    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    protected String accountId;

    @Column(name = "holder_name")
    protected String holderName;

    protected BigDecimal balance;
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("SAVINGS")
public class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Column(name = "interest_rate")
    private BigDecimal interestRate;
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("CURRENT")
public class CurrentAccount extends Account {
    @Column(name = "overdraft_limit")
    private BigDecimal overdraftLimit;
}
```

Now every account entity inherits audit columns and lifecycle callbacks from `Auditable`, and you can still query `Account` polymorphically to get both savings and current accounts.

### When should you use @MappedSuperclass?

Use @MappedSuperclass when:

- You have common columns (audit fields, version, soft-delete flag) repeated across many unrelated entities.
- You never need to query the base type directly.
- You never need a relationship pointing to the base type.
- You want each entity to have its own independent, clean table.

At National Bank of India, Pankaj uses a `BaseEntity` mapped superclass with `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `createdBy`, and `version` fields. Every entity in the system extends it, getting automatic audit stamping with zero overhead.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.time.LocalDateTime;

@MappedSuperclass
public abstract class BaseEntity {

    @Column(name = "created_at", updatable = false)
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @Column(name = "updated_at")
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;

    @PrePersist
    protected void prePersist() {
        this.createdAt = LocalDateTime.now();
        this.updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }

    @PreUpdate
    protected void preUpdate() {
        this.updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }
}
```

### When should you NOT use @MappedSuperclass?

Do not use @MappedSuperclass when:

- The subtypes are genuinely different kinds of the same domain concept (SavingsAccount and CurrentAccount are kinds of Account).
- You need polymorphic queries ("find all accounts regardless of type").
- You need polymorphic associations (`@ManyToOne Account` that can hold any subtype).
- You need runtime type dispatch in business logic.

In all these cases, use @Inheritance instead.

```java
// You need to list all accounts regardless of type
// MappedSuperclass CANNOT do this
// Inheritance CAN do this
List<Account> allAccounts = em.createQuery(
    "SELECT a FROM Account a WHERE a.balance > :min", Account.class)
    .setParameter("min", new BigDecimal("10000.00"))
    .getResultList();
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **@MappedSuperclass and Spring Data JPA.** Spring Data's `JpaRepository<Account, String>` works with entities, not with mapped superclasses. You cannot create `JpaRepository<Auditable, Long>`.
- **Combining layers.** The common production pattern is @MappedSuperclass for audit/base fields at the top, then @Inheritance hierarchies where the domain requires polymorphism.
- **@AttributeOverride on inherited fields.** A subclass of @MappedSuperclass can use `@AttributeOverride` to rename inherited columns. This is useful when two subclasses need different column names for the same field.
- **Performance.** @MappedSuperclass adds zero runtime overhead. It is purely a compile-time mapping mechanism. @Inheritance has performance implications depending on the strategy chosen.
- **Testing.** When writing integration tests, you cannot persist or query the @MappedSuperclass directly. Always test through concrete entity subclasses.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.SavingsAccount;
import com.bank.model.CurrentAccount;
import jakarta.persistence.EntityManager;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;

public class AccountReportService {

    private final EntityManager em;

    public AccountReportService(EntityManager em) { this.em = em; }

    public void generateReport() {
        // Polymorphic query -- only possible with @Inheritance
        List<Account> accounts = em.createQuery(
            "SELECT a FROM Account a", Account.class)
            .getResultList();

        for (Account acc : accounts) {
            if (acc instanceof SavingsAccount sa) {
                System.out.println("Savings: " + sa.getAccountId()
                    + " rate=" + sa.getInterestRate());
            } else if (acc instanceof CurrentAccount ca) {
                System.out.println("Current: " + ca.getAccountId()
                    + " limit=" + ca.getOverdraftLimit());
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Savings: SAV2001 rate=6.50
Current: CUR3001 limit=50000.00
```
